# No “Offline” product naming (hard rule)

“Offline” is a **deprecated product term**. It must not appear in new
repository names, crate names intended as product surface, documentation
titles, UI strings, partner copy, or forward-looking architecture as a
product brand. It is also **not** the word for machine connectivity.

## The rule

> **Do not create new repos, crates, docs titles, UI strings, or partner
> language that brand the product as “Offline PIM”, “AIC Offline”, or
> “Offline Server”. Use AIC Server, enclave, agent, recording proxy / jump
> host capacity, and related plain US English.**
>
> **Air-gapped is the machine-state word** — the system is isolated from
> general network reach (never connected, or not generally connected).
> The **Agent is described by function** (rotate credentials, propagate
> changes, handle validated elevation) on **air-gapped and online**
> systems alike. Do not call a machine “offline” or “offline-only.”
> Do not define the Agent by whether it can reach the server.

## Allowed uses of “offline”

| Allowed | Example |
| --- | --- |
| Historical git / path names that already exist | `pim-offline-server` repo (do not rename); `pim-orm-offline` crate |
| Legacy SQL table prefix | `offline_*` columns/tables in `pim-orm-offline` — **schema namespace only**, not product branding or machine state |
| Existing binary / program ids already shipped | `pim-offline-agent`, `pim-offline-server` program ids until a deliberate rename program |
| Powered-off host state in operator copy | “Sleeping or offline” meaning the seat is powered off — not air-gapped |
| Viewing a file without a network | “Export to PDF for offline viewing” |

## Forbidden in new work

- New GitHub repos named `*-offline-*` for product features (bad example already
  shipped: `analoginfo-pim/orm-offline-local-patches` — prefer rename guidance
  when touching; do not create more).
- New crate names like `pim-offline-vm-*` for greenfield work — prefer
  `pim-vm-hypervisor`, `vm_scheduler`, etc.
- Doc / UI titles: “AIC Offline”, “Offline PIM Server”, “Offline Workstations”.
- Partner or assessor prose that calls the control plane “the Offline product”.
- Operator chrome that calls a machine “offline-only” or “disconnected” when
  the meaning is **air-gapped**.
- Defining the Agent as “the offline agent” instead of naming rotate /
  propagate / elevation.

## Preferred vocabulary

| Concept | Prefer |
| --- | --- |
| Control plane binary / product | **AIC Server** (Help → About only for the marketed toolkit name) |
| CMMC / MSP boundary | **enclave** |
| Endpoint client | **Agent** — rotate, propagate, validated elevation; air-gapped **and** online |
| Machine connectivity | **air-gapped** or **online** (never `offline` as a connectivity enum) |
| Wire profile we own | `deployment_profile`: write `air_gapped`; dual-read legacy `disconnected` |
| Heavy session capture host | **recording proxy** / **jump host capacity** |
| Seat VM power control | **VM scheduler**, **My Workstations** power state |
| Hypervisor adapter crate | `pim-vm-hypervisor` |

## SQL namespace note

Tables may keep the historical `offline_*` prefix when matching
`pim-orm-offline` convention (for example `offline_vm_pool`). Document in the
migration and feature doc that this prefix is a **legacy schema namespace**,
not the product name and not machine connectivity. Prefer `enclave_*` for
greenfield inventory-adjacent tables when that matches nearby tables; do not
invent new *product* names containing Offline.

## Agent duty

1. When writing new docs or UI copy — strip “Offline” product branding and
   use **air-gapped** for machine state. Describe the Agent by function.
2. Mirror this rule to `core-assets/.cursor/rules/no-offline-product-naming.mdc`
   in the same change set (workspace `.cursor` is not a git root).
3. Do not rename the historical `pim-offline-server` git repository in this
   rule’s scope; that is a separate migration program.
4. Do not rename `aic-offline-agent-service`, the `offline_*` SQL prefix, or
   `/api/offline/*` route paths in this rule’s scope.

